WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
Speaker 1: if you've been watching the show&nbsp;
for a while or if you're just aware of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:07.760
American system of government more generally,&nbsp;
then you'll know what gerrymandering is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:13.040
Very simply, it's when state legislatures draw&nbsp;
up district maps in a way that will benefit them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.520
The party that's already in control gets to do&nbsp;
this, so it's effectively a way for politicians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.640
to choose their voter. Rather than voters&nbsp;
choosing their politicians. So, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:28.080
if you're a Republican state, but you have a big&nbsp;
Democratic city in the state and let's say you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.840
awarded five districts from the census that gets&nbsp;
done every ten years, you may decide to make the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.640
city one district with the more rural areas&nbsp;
for separate districts. So then you'll have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:45.120
4 to 1 advantage. Or if you can get away with it,&nbsp;
maybe you'll break up that city into five pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:50.080
combining those pieces with the more rural&nbsp;
areas that may outweigh the parts of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:55.040
city in population. So then you can get a 5 to&nbsp;
0 advantage. That's kind of how it works. It's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:00.640
practice that really has to be done away with, and&nbsp;
there are laws against going too far with it, like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:06.320
by breaking up districts by ethnic neighborhoods.&nbsp;
You can't you can't do too much with that. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:11.200
districts have to be contiguous. But the states&nbsp;
over the years have been able to get away with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:16.560
quite a lot as it pertains to redistricting.&nbsp;
Like, for example, let's take a look at this map.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:22.560
Here is a picture of Dan Crenshaw's oddly shaped&nbsp;
district in the Houston area. It looks like maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.120
a salamander, if you can say that it resembles&nbsp;
anything at all. So this is some pretty extreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.920
gerrymandering here. Well, every now and then,&nbsp;
the courts will step in and strike down one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.160
these egregiously gerrymandered maps, which is&nbsp;
good to see, because that means that there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:41.280
some accountability at least. But the problem is&nbsp;
that it's often too little, too late. And we may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.560
go through an election cycle or two or three&nbsp;
or four before these illegally drawn districts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.120
can be corrected. And that's exactly the situation&nbsp;
that we're finding ourselves in now going into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.480
2022 midterms. Since January, district maps in the&nbsp;
Republican controlled states of Alabama, Georgia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:01.600
Louisiana and Ohio have been deemed unlawful. But&nbsp;
rather than putting in an immediate stop to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:05.760
they've been allowed to go forward for this&nbsp;
election cycle. The states are challenging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:11.120
the respective judge's rulings, and the plan is&nbsp;
to just figure it all out later. And it's not an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.680
insignificant number of seats, too. It's about 10%&nbsp;
of seats in the House of Representatives that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:22.240
affected by this ruling. And it's going to end up&nbsp;
awarding Republicans about 5 to 7 seats that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:26.160
otherwise wouldn't be able to win. They're just&nbsp;
going to be essentially entitled to these seats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:31.520
Keep in mind, Republicans only need to pick up&nbsp;
about ten seats or even fewer potentially to win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:37.440
back the House in 2022. So this is definitely the&nbsp;
type of thing that we need emergency relief on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.680
right? We need the Supreme Court to step in and&nbsp;
say, no, you can't use these maps. They've been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:46.640
determined to be illegal and you can't use it&nbsp;
for the midterms. But predictably, they're quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:52.160
laxed on this issue. The Supreme Court is very&nbsp;
conservative right now, 6 to 3 conservative.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:57.360
And this is the type of ruling that's going to&nbsp;
benefit them and their political side in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.760
meantime. So they're happy to see it be allowed&nbsp;
to be used because they know that it's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.240
increase their power. So they're totally fine&nbsp;
with it. They're not going to step in. And it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:12.640
yet another example of the 2016 election rearing&nbsp;
its ugly head and how Donald Trump, being able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:18.080
appoint those three Supreme Court justices has put&nbsp;
us in a terrible position as a country. And it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:23.360
also part of the reason why FiveThirtyEight right&nbsp;
now expects that Republicans have about a 79 80%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:28.160
chance of winning back the lower chamber&nbsp;
in 2022. Sure, it's not just this issue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:32.240
it's other things, too. Like we probably should&nbsp;
have picked up more seats in the House in 2020.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:37.600
There's also the historical trend of how the party&nbsp;
in charge that has the White House tends to have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.800
bad first midterm. So there's other things working&nbsp;
against us. Sure. But also, gerrymandering plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.360
a significant role. And it's really something&nbsp;
that we cannot have in a functioning democracy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.520
something that we have to work on and we&nbsp;
have to fix this process or at least have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:56.080
a way to undo these maps immediately when they're&nbsp;
determined to be illegal. All right. That's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:01.600
to do it for today's episode of The David Pakman&nbsp;
Show. My thank you to David for allowing me to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:07.360
fill in today as he is up in Canada at the moment.&nbsp;
Have a great weekend, everybody. And I'm going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.720
be back on Monday hosting once again.
Speaker 6: See you then.

